Title: Allow cross-building Windows binaries from a Linux host
Origin: electron-legacy (this repo)

Applied from src/ after the chromium/, electron/ and node/ series, so it can
adjust code those add.

Chromium cross builds compile some targets twice: once with the host toolchain
(Linux) for build-time tools, once with the target toolchain (Windows) for what
ships. Several imported patches restore Windows-only code into files that build
on every platform, which is invisible when the host is Windows and a compile
error when it is not:

  base/files/file_util.cc        chromium/base/0324 -- upstream changed
                                 file_pre_reader_win.cc, a Windows-only file;
                                 ours targets file_util.cc. Guards the restored
                                 code and makes the XP path an early return.
  net/base/ip_endpoint.cc        chromium/net/0070 -- resolves if_indextoname
                                 from Iphlpapi.dll because XP lacks it.
  .../quiche/quiche_ip_address.cc chromium/net/0251 -- inet_ntop/inet_pton
                                 replacements for XP, reading the Win32 in_addr
                                 layout (S_un). Uses quiche's own
                                 #if defined(_WIN32), as quiche does not use
                                 Chromium's BUILDFLAG.

It also fixes one Electron target that cannot work in a cross build at all:
mksnapshot_checksum_gen hashes the v8 context snapshot, but Chromium disables
that snapshot for Windows and macOS cross builds (building Blink twice is slow,
see tools/v8_context_snapshot/v8_context_snapshot.gni). The target was defined
whenever current_toolchain == default_toolchain while only being depended on
under the full condition, and GN validates every defined target, so gn gen
failed with "Input to target not generated by a dependency". Both now use one
enable_v8_context_snapshot_checksum flag, and the two places that read
snapshot_checksum.h are guarded by it. Note release builds set
use_v8_context_snapshot = true explicitly (see assets/args), which turns the
checksum back on and accepts the second Blink build.

